A prefix sum array is a powerful technique in computer science used to efficiently compute the sum of elements in a range of an array. Here's a breakdown:

🔹 What is a Prefix Sum Array?
Given an array A of length n, the prefix sum array P is another array where each element at index i is the sum of all elements in A from index 0 to i.
P[0] = A[0]
P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i]  for i > 0
This allows you to quickly compute the sum of any subarray A[l..r] (where l <= r) in constant time:
🔹 Example
Suppose:
A = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5]
Then:
P = [3, 4, 8, 9, 14]
To get the sum from index 1 to 3 (i.e., 1 + 4 + 1 = 6):
P[3] - P[0] = 9 - 3 = 6
